    and the Rotary menu, is docs/moderator-guide.md in the website's repository. -#}
<div class="wrap section narrow prose">
  <h1>Moderator guide</h1>
  <p class="lead">You check what clubs and members send before it appears on the district website. Everything is done on your phone, on the <a href="/desk/">moderation desk</a>. You do not need Google Sheets.</p>

  <h2 id="start">Getting started</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the desk from the <strong>Open the moderation desk</strong> button in any moderator email. After the first time, your phone remembers it.</li>
    <li>Type your email address (the one the district has on its moderators list) and tap <strong>Send me a code</strong>.</li>
    <li>An email arrives with a 6-digit code and a <strong>Sign in</strong> button. Use either within 15 minutes.</li>
    <li>You stay signed in on that phone for 7 days. On someone else's phone, tap <strong>Sign out</strong> when you finish.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The <strong>हिन्दी</strong> button at the top switches the desk's own words to Hindi. What clubs typed stays as they typed it.</p>

  <h2 id="rules">Three rules for everyone</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Your name stays private.</strong> Answer the public and clubs only from the desk, never from your own email or phone. The desk sends from the district account, signed "Rotary District 3110".</li>
    <li><strong>Nothing personal goes on the website.</strong> No personal mobile numbers or emails, no UPI or bank details, no children's names. Tap <strong>Correct</strong> to remove them, or reject.</li>
    <li><strong>When unsure, reject kindly.</strong> Write a reason the sender can act on ("Please send a photo of the work itself"). They can send it again.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="content">Content moderators</h2>
  <p>You check activities, events, club profile updates and public services (blood banks, eye care and so on). Please try to check each item within 48 hours.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>From the email:</strong> each new item sends you an email with its text, photos and two big buttons, <strong>Approve</strong> and <strong>Reject</strong>. They open the desk, where one more tap confirms. That extra tap is on purpose.</li>
    <li><strong>On the desk, To check:</strong> every waiting item as a card. Tap a photo to see it larger. Read the notes: they warn about possible duplicates, a missing photo, a future date or payment details.</li>
    <li><strong>Approve</strong> when it is a real club activity, with a suitable photo or a link to the club's own post, and the photo-consent box is ticked.</li>
    <li><strong>Correct</strong> fixes spelling, shortens text, or removes a phone number before you approve. Do not rewrite the club's story.</li>
    <li><strong>Reject</strong> needs a reason. Tap one of the ready-made reasons or write your own. The club gets it by email.</li>
    <li><strong>Recent:</strong> approved activities and coming events. <strong>★ Story of the month</strong> marks the best activity of the month. <strong>Leave out of bulletin</strong> keeps an item out of Sunday's bulletin.</li>
    <li><strong>Saturday 6 pm:</strong> an email shows tomorrow's bulletin. Nothing to do if it looks right. Tap <strong>Leave this out</strong> next to anything that should not go out.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If someone else got there first, the desk says so ("Already approved by …").</p>

  <h2 id="members">Members moderators</h2>
  <p>You check members' consent forms (directory, photo, birthday and anniversary greetings) and removal requests. These are never approved from an email, only on the signed-in desk.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Consent forms:</strong> check that the photo shows the member alone and is suitable, and read the warnings (declaration not ticked, spouse's agreement missing). Then <strong>Approve</strong> or <strong>Reject</strong> with a reason.</li>
    <li><strong>Removal requests:</strong> tap <strong>Remove now</strong>, within two days. The website updates within about 30 minutes.</li>
    <li><strong>Mark as deceased:</strong> search by name, check it is the right person and club, then confirm. Greetings stop at once.</li>
    <li><strong>Couples waiting:</strong> anniversaries waiting for the other spouse's form. Ask the club to follow up.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="clubs">Club engagement moderators</h2>
  <p>You encourage clubs that have not posted for a while.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Quiet clubs</strong> lists active clubs with no activity in the last 30 days, with the last activity date and who reminded them last.</li>
    <li><strong>Email President and Secretary</strong> sends a friendly reminder with the club's own form link, from the district account and signed "Rotary District 3110 website team". A hidden copy comes to you. Once a week at most per club.</li>
    <li><strong>WhatsApp</strong> opens WhatsApp with a ready message and the club's form link. You choose the person to send it to.</li>
    <li>On the 1st of each month an email lists the clubs that did not post in the month just ended.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="office">District office moderators</h2>
  <p>You answer messages from the website's contact form.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Each message comes to you by email. <strong>Do not press Reply on that email</strong>: your own name and email would show.</li>
    <li>On the desk, open <strong>Messages</strong>, tap <strong>Reply</strong> on the message, type your answer and tap <strong>Send</strong>. It goes from the district account, signed "Rotary District 3110", and the message is marked answered. Your answer is kept with the message.</li>
    <li>Someone who gave only a mobile number: call or WhatsApp them from the <strong>district office phone</strong>, not your personal phone. Then tap <strong>Mark answered</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Sunday 8 am:</strong> an email brings the week's bulletin message. Forward it to the district WhatsApp groups.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="practice">The practice round</h2>
  <p>Before the website opens, the administrator starts a practice round. You get an email listing what to try, and practice items made for you appear on the desk, with titles starting "Practice:". They look real, but they are never published, and nobody outside the moderators is emailed.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Content:</strong> approve the tree plantation (try the Approve button in its email); remove the mobile number from the blood donation camp with Correct, then approve; change the dinner's fee to "₹300 per person" with Correct, then approve; reject the old-age home visit (no photo or link) with a reason.</li>
    <li><strong>Members:</strong> reject the consent form of "Practice: Rtn Asha Sharma" (declaration not ticked) with a reason. Then search in Mark as deceased, and do not confirm.</li>
    <li><strong>District office:</strong> reply to "Practice: Ramesh Gupta"; the answer comes to your own inbox, exactly as the public would get it. "Practice: Sunita" gave only a mobile number: do not call it (it is not real), just tap Mark answered.</li>
    <li><strong>Club engagement:</strong> in Quiet clubs, tap WhatsApp for any club and send the message to yourself. Do not tap Email: it really emails the club.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>When you have finished, tell the website administrator.</p>

  <h2 id="help">Something not working?</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>No code arrives:</strong> look in Spam, check the spelling, and use the email the district has for you. Still nothing: ask the administrator to check the moderators list.</li>
    <li><strong>A link has expired:</strong> email buttons work for 7 days. Open the desk instead; the item is under To check.</li>
    <li><strong>Anything else:</strong> tell the website administrator what you tapped and what the desk said.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><a class="btn btn-gold" href="/desk/">Open the moderation desk</a></p>
</div>
